<DocShell
   title="Verification semantics"
   summary="This is the page that keeps the product honest. Verification is allowed to say pass, fail, mixed, manual-only, or not-covered. It is not allowed to bluff."
   links={links}
>
   <section>
      <h2>Three evidence lanes</h2>
      <ul>
         <li><strong>Automated:</strong> the runtime has enough procedure coverage to make a machine verdict.</li>
         <li><strong>Hybrid:</strong> the runtime can gather evidence, but a human still has to interpret part of it.</li>
         <li><strong>Manual:</strong> the runtime can explain the criterion and maybe collect context, but it cannot finish the judgment alone.</li>
      </ul>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2>Level verification</h2>
      <p>
         <code>verify level</code> always emits the full criterion matrix. That is deliberate. A conformance
         target like AA is not one verdict. It is a table of rows, and some of those rows may still be
         manual or uncovered even when the automated slice looks clean.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="callout">
      <h2>What not to say</h2>
      <p>
         Do not turn a clean axe run into "WCAG AA compliant." Do not turn a virtual-reader transcript
         into a VoiceOver or NVDA claim. Keep the evidence type and the claim size matched.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
